push costs onto MF holders. Conversely, in-kind ETF redemptions can remove low-basis lots that benefit all holders.\n\n**Monitoring categories**\n- **Transaction costs:** commissions, spreads, and market impact, allocated by originating class.\n- **Cash:** redemption reserves, cash drag, and cash-creation costs.\n- **Gains:** realized gains, lot selection, in-kind offsets, and distributions.\n\n**Illustrative example:** A large MF redemption forces sale of a low-basis lot, distributing gains to remaining holders.\n\n**Order-level framework:** Tag each order by originating class and flow type, allocate fills with documented causal rationale, compare realized gains by class, and reconcile cash to pending flows. Specific tolerances should come from board-approved policy, not universal standards.","ttft_ms":1938,"type":"result","duration_ms":3077,"uuid":"7a35f57a-c790-4a3f-aeae-052ff2b20303","ttft_stream_ms":422,"time_to_request_ms":60,"first_content_frame_ms":422,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
